# KT: End-to-End Understanding of the Release Branching Strategy

## Objective

The objective of this branching strategy is to:
1. Allow continuous feature development.
2. Freeze a stable release for SIT/UAT testing.
3. Fix production/UAT bugs without affecting ongoing development.
4. Ensure all bug fixes are carried forward into future releases.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BRANCHES

Feature Branch
    │
    ▼
Development (Dev)
    │
    ▼
Release Branch
    │
    ▼
SIT
    │
    ▼
UAT
    │
    ▼
Master/Main
    │
    ▼
Production

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 1 - FEATURE DEVELOPMENT

• Every developer creates a feature branch from the Development branch.

Example:

Development
    ├── feature-login
    ├── feature-payment
    ├── feature-profile

Developers work independently.

Once development is completed,

Feature Branch
        │
        ▼
Development

Only reviewed code is merged into Development.

Development now contains:

Sprint 1
Sprint 2
Sprint 3

Development is always the integration branch.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 2 - CREATE RELEASE

Once Sprint 1, Sprint 2 and Sprint 3 are completed,

Create

release/1.0.0

This release branch is a snapshot of Development at that point.

Example:

Development
    Sprint1
    Sprint2
    Sprint3

↓

release/1.0.0

After this,

NO NEW FEATURES should be added to the release branch.

Only

• Bug Fixes
• Version Changes
• Configuration Changes

are allowed.

The release branch becomes frozen.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 3 - CONTINUE DEVELOPMENT

Developers DO NOT STOP.

They continue working on Development.

Development becomes

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Sprint4
Sprint5

Release branch still contains only

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3

Therefore,

Development != Release

This is intentional.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 4 - SIT VALIDATION

Deploy

release/1.0.0

to SIT.

SIT validates

• Functional Testing
• Regression
• Integration

If everything is good,

Create Tag

Tag 1.0.0

Tagging means this exact code version is approved.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 5 - UAT DEPLOYMENT

Deploy the SAME tagged release to UAT.

Important:

Do NOT rebuild.

Do NOT recreate image.

Do NOT rebuild binaries.

Exactly the same artifact/image/tag moves forward.

Flow

release/1.0.0
      │
      ▼
SIT
      │
Tag 1.0.0
      │
      ▼
UAT

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 6 - UAT BUG

Suppose UAT finds a bug.

DO NOT FIX IT IN DEVELOPMENT.

Instead,

Create

release/1.0.0.1

(or continue working in the existing release branch depending on company policy.)

Now the release contains

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Bug Fix

Notice

Sprint4
Sprint5

are NOT included.

This keeps the release stable.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 7 - VALIDATE THE FIX

According to the process shared in the email,

Deploy

release/1.0.0.1

to SIT.

Validate the bug fix.

If successful,

Promote the SAME release

to UAT.

Flow

release/1.0.0.1
        │
        ▼
SIT
        │
        ▼
UAT

No rebuild.

No cherry-picking.

Same release.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 8 - BACK MERGE

This is the MOST IMPORTANT STEP.

After UAT approves,

Merge

release/1.0.0.1

BACK

into

Development.

Example

Development

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Sprint4
Sprint5

↓

Merge release/1.0.0.1

↓

Development

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Sprint4
Sprint5
Bug Fix

Now the bug fix becomes part of future development.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

WHY BACK MERGE IS NECESSARY

Suppose we DO NOT merge back.

Development

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Sprint4
Sprint5

Release

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Bug Fix

Now later,

Create

release/1.0.2

from Development.

Development never received the Bug Fix.

Therefore,

release/1.0.2

contains

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Sprint4
Sprint5

BUT

the previous UAT bug comes back again.

This is called

Branch Divergence

Back merging completely avoids this problem.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 9 - NEXT RELEASE

After Sprint4 and Sprint5 finish,

Create

release/1.0.2

from Development.

Since Development already received

Bug Fix

through back merge,

release/1.0.2 contains

Sprint1
Sprint2
Sprint3
Bug Fix
Sprint4
Sprint5

Everything is preserved.

