QUESTIONS DISCUSSED DURING KT

Q1. Can Sprint4 and Sprint5 continue while Sprint1-3 go to UAT?

Answer:
Yes.
That is exactly why the Release branch exists.
Development continues independently while Release remains frozen.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Where should UAT bugs be fixed?

Answer:
Always in the Release branch.

Never directly in Development.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. After the UAT bug is fixed, where should the code be merged?

Answer:
Merge the Release branch back into the Development branch.

This is called Back Merge.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Why do we back merge?

Answer:
To ensure future releases also contain the UAT bug fixes.

Otherwise, future releases may reintroduce already-fixed defects.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Why not fix directly in Development?

Answer:
Development already contains Sprint4, Sprint5 and other future work.

Fixing there would make it impossible to release only Sprint1-3.

Release must remain isolated.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Is this GitFlow?

Answer:
Yes.

This is a standard GitFlow-based release management process.

GitFlow principles followed:

• Feature branches from Development
• Release branch created from Development
• Development continues independently
• Only bug fixes allowed in Release
• Release promoted through SIT → UAT → Production
• Release bug fixes back merged into Development
• Future releases created from updated Development

